"""
Real-time pulse extraction using the POS algorithm.

pos_algorithm(), bandpass_filter(), estimate_heart_rate(), calculate_snr(),
mean_skin_rgb(), landmarks_to_pixels(), build_face_landmarker(), and
ensure_face_landmarker_model() are copied VERBATIM and never touched by
any of the tracker/diagnostics work below. compute_roi_rects(),
_mask_from_rects(), and build_roi_mask() (the four-patch physiological
ROI: forehead/left cheek/right cheek/chin) are also unchanged.

# ===== TRACKING ARCHITECTURE CHANGE: KLT + ECC -> ORB + RANSAC =====
KLT (cv2.calcOpticalFlowPyrLK / cv2.goodFeaturesToTrack) and ECC
(cv2.findTransformECC) have been removed completely. Motion tracking is
now:

    MediaPipe init -> physiological ROI definition -> ORB feature
    detection (expanded search region around the ROI, never the whole
    frame) -> ORB descriptor matching (previous frame vs current frame)
    -> RANSAC outlier rejection (cv2.estimateAffinePartial2D) -> dominant
    motion from RANSAC inliers -> normalized_motion = median inlier
    displacement / ROI diagonal -> motion-threshold decision -> update ROI
    coordinates -> extract ORIGINAL raw RGB pixels at the new ROI -> POS.

  - MediaPipe runs only for the INITIAL detection and for genuine
    RECOVERY: too few ORB keypoints/matches, RANSAC failing to fit a
    model or having too few inliers, or the resulting ROI leaving the
    frame (and, ADDED, only after the global ORB passport recovery below
    has also failed). It is never triggered merely because
    normalized_motion is above the threshold.
  - ORB (cv2.ORB_create) detects+describes keypoints inside a rectangle
    expanded around the last known ROI location (ORB_SEARCH_PADDING_RATIO),
    never the whole frame/face -- this is a MOTION-ESTIMATION region only,
    entirely separate from the four physiological ROI patches that
    mean_skin_rgb() reads pixels from. ORB features never select rPPG
    pixels.
  - Matches (cv2.BFMatcher, NORM_HAMMING) between the previous frame's
    ORB descriptors and the current frame's feed cv2.estimateAffinePartial2D
    with RANSAC, which both fits a similarity transform (translation +
    rotation + uniform scale) AND flags inlier/outlier matches -- outliers
    are discarded before any motion is measured, exactly as required.
  - The dominant-motion metric used for the threshold decision is the
    MEDIAN of the individual RANSAC-inlier displacement magnitudes (not
    the median of a vector's components) normalized by the ROI diagonal
    (sqrt(w^2+h^2) of the union of the four ROI patches, fixed at the
    last MediaPipe (re)detection until the next one):
        normalized_motion = median(||curr_inlier - prev_inlier||) / roi_diagonal
  - normalized_motion <= MOTION_THRESHOLD (a fraction of the ROI diagonal;
    NOT a fixed pixel count) -> accept the RANSAC-estimated transform
    directly and update the ROI coordinates.
  - normalized_motion > MOTION_THRESHOLD -> the same transform is only
    accepted if RANSAC found enough inliers to trust it at this larger
    scale (MIN_RANSAC_INLIERS_LARGE_MOTION, stricter than the normal-
    motion minimum) AND the resulting ROI stays in bounds; otherwise
    this triggers recovery. This is the "extra validation for large
    motion" -- RANSAC's own inlier count is the confidence signal, no
    separate mechanism is introduced.
  - ROI coordinates are updated algebraically (transform the rectangle) --
    the RGB frame itself is NEVER warped, remapped, or interpolated. After
    the ROI moves, pixels are re-read fresh from the raw current frame by
    the unmodified mean_skin_rgb().

Whichever mechanism supplied the current frame's ROI (MediaPipe or ORB),
the actual rPPG pixel values are always read fresh, unmodified, from the
raw current frame at the final ROI location.

# ===== ADDED: motion/ROI robustness pass (integrated from the aligned
# variant of this script; the ROI-FOLLOWING approach above stays the base) =====
#   1. Spatially distributed ORB: keypoints are bucketed into a 4x4 grid
#      over the existing search region (top-N per cell) so they can't
#      cluster in one spot. (The grid is ONLY for ORB keypoints -- it is
#      NOT the physiological ROI.)
#   2. A facial dynamic-zone mask (from MediaPipe landmarks) keeps ORB
#      keypoints off the eyes/eyebrows/lips and prefers forehead/nose/
#      cheek/jaw.
#   3. Reference-coordinate ROI following: the four physiological patches
#      are frozen at each MediaPipe (re)detection as `reference_rois`, and
#      one cumulative REFERENCE -> CURRENT similarity transform is chained
#      from each per-frame RANSAC transform. The ROI rectangles for the
#      current frame are ALWAYS computed as  reference_rois mapped through
#      that single transform, so (a) every patch keeps its exact relative
#      position/size on the face, (b) per-frame errors are not compounded
#      by repeatedly re-deriving rectangles from rectangles, and (c) the
#      SAME transform is used for small and large motion. The frame is
#      never warped, so there is no double compensation.
#   4. Per-patch physiological-ROI quality monitoring: unreliable patches
#      (out of frame / mostly non-skin) are excluded from the mask handed
#      to the unchanged mean_skin_rgb(), falling back to the original
#      four-patch union if every patch looks unreliable.
#   5. A global ORB "reference passport": a bounded set of descriptors
#      captured once per MediaPipe (re)detection, used ONLY as a recovery
#      path when local ORB tracking fails, before MediaPipe recovery.
# None of this changes POS, filtering, HR estimation, SNR, or the four
# physiological ROI's geometry.
#
# ===== ADDED: KLT frame-to-frame tracking layer (on top of ORB + RANSAC) =====
#   KLT (cv2.calcOpticalFlowPyrLK) = fast frame-to-frame propagation of the
#       previous ORB keypoints (the 4x4-grid, dynamic-zone-masked set).
#   ORB descriptors = appearance validation of every tracked point
#       (Hamming distance + ambiguity ratio test); KLT is never trusted alone.
#   RANSAC (the existing cv2.estimateAffinePartial2D similarity fit) = the
#       geometric outlier rejection + motion estimate, followed by the
#       unchanged reference->current transform chaining (T_new = M @ T_old).
# If KLT/validation yields too few correspondences, the pre-existing
# detect-ORB-and-match path is used for that frame, then the passport, then
# MediaPipe (all unchanged). ORB re-detection is only run when the surviving
# track count drops below KLT_REDETECT_RATIO of the count at the last
# (re)detection.
# ===============================================================

# ===== PERFORMANCE DIAGNOSTICS =====
The 10-second-window summary keeps the original format and reports:
MediaPipe/ORB frames & usage %, MediaPipe re-detections, ORB failures,
RANSAC failures, valid ORB matches, RANSAC inliers, median displacement,
normalized motion, motion classification (SMALL/LARGE), actual FPS, HR,
SNR. All old KLT/ECC-specific statistics have been removed. The SMALL/LARGE
classification now uses the SAME per-frame comparison and the SAME
MOTION_THRESHOLD as the tracker's own decision.

Requires: pip install mediapipe opencv-python scipy numpy
(ORB and estimateAffinePartial2D both ship in core opencv-python --
no opencv-contrib-python required.)
"""

